<h2>Locate Unit Fractions on the Number Line</h2>
<p>Source warm-up: four original representations A, B, C and D of thirds and fourths. Different pictures can be the odd one out under different valid mathematical explanations; the teacher checks discussion.</p>
<h3>Who divided into fourths correctly?</h3>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Original pupil</th><th scope="col">Mathematical reasoning</th><th scope="col">Teacher judgment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Clare</strong></td><td>Divided the full line from 0 to 2 into 4 equal intervals, each is 1/2 of the unit.</td><td>Not fourths of the 0-to-1 unit</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Andre</strong></td><td>Divided 0 to 1 into 4 equal intervals, first mark is 1/4.</td><td>Source-supported partition</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Diego</strong></td><td>Put 4 ticks inside 0-to-1; created 5 intervals.</td><td>Five spaces are not fourths</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
